# Line endings are pinned here rather than left to core.autocrlf.
#
# WHAT WAS WRONG. With no .gitattributes and core.autocrlf=true, the working
# tree drifted into a mixed state: `git ls-files --eol` reported 67 tracked text
# files at LF and **24 at CRLF**, which is exactly the "24 modified files,
# 11304 insertions and 11304 deletions" that showed up across three sessions.
# Nothing was actually edited. Whether a file ended up LF or CRLF depended only
# on whether git or an editor wrote it last -- git converts LF to CRLF on
# checkout under autocrlf, while a tool writing the file directly leaves LF.
#
# The index was never the problem: zero tracked files hold CRLF there. So this
# is not a history repair, it is pinning the working tree so it stops drifting.
#
# WHY eol=lf RATHER THAN NATIVE. `text=auto` alone normalises the index but
# leaves the working tree to core.autocrlf, which is per-machine config and is
# therefore the thing that drifted. Pinning the checkout to LF makes the index
# and the working tree byte-identical, so core.autocrlf cannot reintroduce this
# on either platform -- and this repo is developed on macOS as well as Windows.
#
# Nothing in the build needs CRLF: MSVC, CMake and PowerShell 5.1 all read LF
# perfectly well, and there are no .bat or .cmd files, which are the only things
# that genuinely require it. The rule for those is below anyway, so adding one
# later cannot silently get LF.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Named explicitly as well as covered by the rule above. This is the list to
# check when someone asks whether a file type is handled.
*.cpp          text eol=lf
*.h            text eol=lf
*.hlsl         text eol=lf
*.md           text eol=lf
*.ps1          text eol=lf
*.txt          text eol=lf
*.yml          text eol=lf
*.yaml         text eol=lf
*.cmake        text eol=lf
*.qrc          text eol=lf
*.rc           text eol=lf
*.svg          text eol=lf
*.json         text eol=lf
CMakeLists.txt text eol=lf

# The exception, kept ahead of ever needing it: cmd.exe genuinely requires CRLF
# and a batch file checked out with LF fails in ways that look like a bad script.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Binary. Never converted, never diffed as text -- line-ending translation
# applied to an image corrupts it silently, which is the one failure here that
# would not announce itself.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.ico  binary
*.icns binary
*.pdf  binary
*.zip  binary
*.mov  binary
*.mp4  binary
*.exr  binary
